          title: "The end of the book",
          body: "The Gospel signs itself. **“This is the disciple who is bearing witness about these things, and who has written these things”** — the beloved disciple, John, steps out from behind his self-effacing title one last time to vouch for what we have read. This is not legend gathered at a distance; it is *testimony,* the report of a man who leaned on Jesus’ breast, stood at the cross, and looked into the empty tomb — *“and we know that his testimony is true.”* Everything in the Gospel has been aimed at faith (20:31), and faith rests on witness; here is the witness, named and vouched for. And then the last verse lifts its eyes from the page to the immeasurable ocean beyond it. What John has written is true, but it is not *all* — **“there are also many other things that Jesus did,”** so many that *“the world itself could not contain the books that would be written.”** It is a lovely closing hyperbole, but it carries a real truth: no book, no library, no lifetime of study can exhaust him. The Gospel is a doorway, not a boundary; it tells us enough to believe and to live, and then points past its own last word to a Lord whose fullness runs on beyond every telling. The story that opened *“In the beginning was the Word”* closes still open — because the One it is about is alive, and his story has no end.",
